{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a strong downtrend (e.g., falling from ~80 to ~65) followed by a model forecast that is flat or slowly rising, apply a downward correction bias of 3-5% because the true outcome often continues lower than the smoothed forecast.",
    "If sentiment history contains isolated spikes (1.00) amidst low average sentiment, do not overweight these in the forecast; instead, assume the market will revert to the mean defined by the recent price range rather than following the spike direction.",
    "When the model predicts a steady upward trend while the last 5 days of history show high volatility and no clear directional momentum, expect an overshoot error where true outcomes drop significantly below the forecasted path within the first 10 days.",
    "In volatile regimes (history range >20 points), reduce the forecast horizon's expected growth rate by approximately 40% to account for mean-reversion behavior that standard models often miss.",
    "If sentiment history is predominantly neutral (0.00) but price action shows a recent reversal, the model's continuation bias will likely fail; apply a correction factor that weights the most recent price change more heavily than the forecasted trend."
  ]
}
